you were at the game just tell us about  your memories of the match and your  feelings about it now as a survivor well  it's it's very mixed feelings you know  today my thoughts are with the families  as always I hope that now they can begin  to get some peace in 27 years of mental  torture I mean [ __ ] for me and for my  two sons and nephew with me on the day  you know it began as a typical classic  outing to a Liverpool match in a in a  cup run and beautiful sunny April day  going over the pen inés arriving at  Leppings Lane to find it seething and  out of control by the turnstiles with no  effort made to stream the supporters and  get them into lines and check the  tickets outside and very frightening I  mean the police actually were helping  people to climb over the walls into the  ground you know but my two sons and  Matthew and Nick and my nephew Karl went  on ahead into the ground because I  didn't like the crush I held back they  went on eventually clearly after the  gates had opened it could calm down and  I went through I didn't know anything  had happened untoward bought a program  and then a man came rushing up to a  policeman he was standing by me and said  you've got to do something this kid's  dying in there  so I made haste up the steps to the to  the seating area above the central pens  be just behind the girl very good seats  couldn't get down to the seats the  aisles were crowned I could see car  lights at the front helping to pull  people up from below we've seen those  scenes on the television and I had to  clamber over the seats to get down to  the to the row near the front where we  had the seats and the game had stopped  by then  almost immediately I saw them bringing  lifeless shapes of bodies out into the  into the penalty area to the six-yard  box and I said to one of my sons I think  that person may be dead and then another  person I think they may be that they're  lifeless they you know maybe they're and  then the tunnel he appealed for medics  and nurses to help so I went immediately  down the back of the steps again  presented myself to a police officer and  said who do I report to what do I do he  said of no idea and then it the complete  chaos became apparent and I finished up  having to initiate the triaging of the  casualties because the police were  beginning to take people out of various  severity z-- to hopefully the ambulances  arriving because they at that time I  think there was only one ambulance there  so I and others who arrived or the  doctor and some nurses systematically  sorted the casualties and sent them off  the ones who really needed to be got to  hospital quickest and we did that and we  did that round and round the rows of  people who were leaning against the  fence until it was all over and I was  then asked to certify dead and I by one  of the policemen I certified six people  dead wrote out impromptu death  certificates with sheets ripped out from  an exercise pad and pinned to the  garland swithed safety pins there was no  ID on on five of the six these are the  days before mobile phones the day before  days before bank cards even and and then  it all likewise I went to the gymnasium  to see what was going on I went twice or  the other but I was surprised to find in  the gymnasium photographs of lots of  Liverpool supporters on the wall that  was a kind of rogues gallery of people  they were expecting to go cause trouble  and it gave you an insight into the  mindset of the police on the day they  saw it as being  about hooliganism about crowd control  abouts Kali's about scousers and all of  that and then at a certain point then I  was cleared out everybody wasn't a  police officer it was cleared out of the  gym they closed the doors and I suspect  that's when the cover-up started when a  narrative that was presented to mrs.  Thatcher and Bernhard Ingram on the  Sunday morning began to be formulated  when I was going back to Leppings Lane  from the GM I met a young policeman  absolutely distraught him in tears and  he said to me the senior officers  they're going to blame us now it was all  their fault but they're going to blame  us because ironically they didn't blame  the junior officers they blamed the  supporters and I got in my car with the  boys drove back to Liverpool went into  Radio City where I used to be regular  health broadcasts because I knew all the  people were you guys and I went on the  evening news and told the story as I had  experienced it because I knew it was  going to be necessary for somebody like  myself a professional to tell the story  to make sure the right story was what  was out there  subsequently Lord Justice Taylor in his  report said that I was totally  irresponsible to criticise the emergency  response he said that I made three  claims that the emergency services were  slow to arrive that there was inadequate  equipment on those ambulances when they  arrived and that if the emergency  response had been better lives might  have been saved Taylor says that I John  Ashton was wrong on all three counts I  lived with that for 24 years 25 years  that I was professionally telling fibs  lying whatever misleading about the  response and you know all that but so I  had to live with that but the important  thing today is the families you know  I've said this many times that if you  lose a loved one a parent a grandparent  when their time has come  that's terrible thing to happen and we  all have to live through that if you  lose somebody through an accident that  must be many times worse but if you lose  somebody through a disaster like this  that could have been avoided if all  these agencies have done their job  properly the FA the engineers the club  the police the ambulance service if  they'd done their job properly it could  have been avoided and then it's followed  up by lies cover-up innuendo character  assassination and so on how do you come  to terms with that how do you come to  terms with that I just hope that after  today the families can begin to get  peace we've got a lot of the facts we  still don't know the level of  involvement of the Cabinet Office we  wait the appearance of the Cabinet  Office papers in a couple of years time  I hope they don't disappear I hope  they're not redacted but we need to know  as well what the involvement of the  government departments of the day and of  Downing Street was in orchestrating the  narrative about what was supposed to  have happened but today's the day to  think about the 96 today's today that I  think about young Phil Hammond who  played football in the same school team  that's my boy Nick and who I and some  other men took the team to to Rennes in  France for a week playing footballer I  think of Phil and I think of Phil's  family and for me that brings the  enormity of these 96 to reality and I  have trouble holding back the tears and  I think about them and I think about the  breakdown of trust with it with the  police force who are supposed to police  by consent and the shocking lack of  shame of people who've lied over all  these years and some of whom still will  not admit their responsibility I think  of that  and I worry about that about this  country about its leadership and about  the fact that people will not accept  responsibility when things go wrong I  hope this is a watershed and I hope one  of the legacies of Hillsboro will be  that when things go wrong people put  their hands up and say that they are  responsible
 

